const title       = 'Raven vs Crow Call: How to Tell Them Apart by Sound';
const description = 'Ravens and crows have entirely different calls — the raven\'s deep "kronk" or "prruk" versus the crow\'s sharp "caw". This page compares raven and crow vocalisations in detail, explaining acoustic differences, context, and how call alone can clinch an identification at any distance.';
const path        = '/compare/raven-vs-crow-calls/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',    url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare', url: '/compare/' },
  { name: 'Raven vs Crow Calls', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What does a raven sound like vs a crow?', a: 'The Common Raven\'s most characteristic call is a deep, resonant "kronk" or "prruk" — a hollow, wooden note in the range of 300–600 Hz, often described as sounding like a large bell struck with a wooden mallet, or like a deep croak. The American Crow or Carrion Crow produces the familiar "caw" — a sharper, higher-pitched call in the 500–1,000 Hz range, with a harsh, thin quality. The raven\'s call is roughly an octave lower and far more resonant. Heard at distance, ravens produce a low, travelling "quork" that carries; crows produce a higher, sharper rattle. Once you know the difference, the two are rarely confused by ear.' },
  { q: 'Can you identify a raven by call alone?', a: 'Yes — an experienced birder can identify a Common Raven by call alone with high confidence. The deep, resonant, single- or double-noted "kronk/prruk/quork" of a raven is acoustically distinctive from any crow species. Ravens also have a wider repertoire of calls than crows: in addition to the contact "kronk", they produce deep gurgles, rattles, bell-like notes, and an extraordinary range of mimicry of other species. When ravens call, the sound has a wooden, hollow, almost bell-like resonance that is physically distinct from the crow\'s thinner, sharper caw. If you hear a crow-like bird and the call is noticeably deeper and more resonant than what you usually hear, it is almost certainly a raven.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens mimic other sounds?', a: 'Ravens are among the most accomplished vocal mimics of any bird. They mimic the calls of other bird species (eagle calls, other corvids), mammalian sounds, and human speech. Wild ravens mimic wolves and foxes — behaviour documented by Bernd Heinrich and others — apparently attracting these predators to carcasses to open them. Captive ravens readily learn human words and can produce remarkably accurate imitations of specific voices. American Crows also mimic, but less extensively than ravens. The raven\'s mimicry extends to non-biological sounds: car engines, doors, bells, and other environmental sounds are recorded from wild and captive birds.' },
];
