    <h1>Raven vs Crow Call: How to Tell Them Apart by Sound</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The raven's call is deeper, more resonant, and lower-pitched than any crow species — a hollow "kronk" or "prruk" around 300–600 Hz versus the crow's sharp "caw" at 500–1,000 Hz. The raven sounds like a bell or a deep wooden knock; the crow sounds sharp and thin. This acoustic difference is consistent, field-reliable, and among experienced birders is often the first identification cue. Ravens also have a richer overall repertoire, including gurgles, rattles, and extensive mimicry.</p>
  </section>

