        <thead>
          <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Common Raven</th><th>Carrion / American Crow</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Primary contact call</td><td>"kronk", "prruk", "quork"</td><td>"caw", "kaah"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pitch</td><td>Low — 300–600 Hz</td><td>Medium-high — 500–1,000 Hz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Resonant, hollow, wooden</td><td>Sharp, thin, nasal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Notes per call</td><td>Typically 1–2</td><td>Typically 1–4 rapid notes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Repertoire size</td><td>Very large (dozens of distinct calls + mimicry)</td><td>Large (many calls, some mimicry)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mimicry of other species</td><td>Extensive, including wolves, eagles, speech</td><td>Moderate — occasional mimicry</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Acoustic Characteristics in Detail</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Spectrographic analysis of raven and crow calls reveals the acoustic basis for the perceived difference:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raven fundamental frequency:</strong> the "kronk" call has a fundamental frequency of approximately 300–500 Hz with strong lower harmonics — this produces the resonant, wooden quality. The low frequency carries well over long distances and through dense forest.</li>
      <li><strong>Crow fundamental frequency:</strong> the "caw" is higher at approximately 500–900 Hz, with less pronounced lower harmonics, producing the characteristic nasal, cutting quality.</li>
      <li><strong>Call duration:</strong> raven contact calls are typically longer and more drawn-out than crow caws; a raven's "prruk" lasts 0.3–0.5 seconds while a crow "caw" is 0.2–0.3 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Structural complexity:</strong> ravens produce calls with more internal frequency modulation — the note rises and falls within the call, contributing to the resonant, bell-like quality. Crow calls are more uniform in pitch within each note.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Call Context and Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both ravens and crows use calls in context-specific ways:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Food calls:</strong> ravens produce specific calls at food sources — including calls that recruit other ravens to a carcass. The yell call and the "food call" have been experimentally shown to differ from alarm and contact calls, and to function as recruitment signals.</li>
      <li><strong>Alarm calls:</strong> both species produce distinct alarm calls that differ from contact calls. Raven alarm calls are typically higher-pitched and more rapid than their contact calls, reducing the acoustic gap with crow alarm calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Territorial/pair calls:</strong> ravens in established pairs use a "knocking" call during territory advertisement — a wooden "tok tok tok" that has no equivalent in crow vocalisations.</li>
      <li><strong>Flock crows:</strong> crows in large groups produce sustained, overlapping cawing — a sound very different from raven flocks, which tend to produce varied individual calls rather than massed cawing.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Using Call for Field Identification</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Call is among the most reliable raven identification tools:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>At distances where visual field marks (tail shape, bill profile) are ambiguous, call remains diagnostic.</li>
      <li>A single "kronk" or "prruk" from an unseen bird overhead is sufficient for confident raven identification in most of Europe and North America.</li>
      <li>Conversely, a persistent "caw caw caw" sequence almost always indicates crow, not raven — ravens rarely produce sustained rapid-note cawing.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

